<article class="env-lemma" id="0C5G">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C5G">Lemma <span data-tag="0C5G">54.3.3</span>.</a> Let $(A, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be a regular local ring of dimension $2$. Let $f : X \to S = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ be the blowing up of $A$ in $\mathfrak m$. Then $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (X) = \mathbf{Z}$ generated by $\mathcal{O}_ X(E)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Recall that $E = \mathbf{P}^1_\kappa $ has Picard group $\mathbf{Z}$ with generator $\mathcal{O}(1)$, see Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BXJ" data-tag="0BXJ">31.29.5</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0AGQ" data-tag="0AGQ">54.3.1</a> the invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module $\mathcal{O}_ X(E)$ restricts to $\mathcal{O}(-1)$. Hence $\mathcal{O}_ X(E)$ generates an infinite cyclic group in $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (X)$. Since $A$ is regular it is a UFD, see More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AG0" data-tag="0AG0">15.123.2</a>. Then the punctured spectrum $U = S \setminus \{ \mathfrak m\}  = X \setminus E$ has trivial Picard group, see Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BDA" data-tag="0BDA">31.29.4</a>. Hence for every invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module $\mathcal{L}$ there is an isomorphism $s : \mathcal{O}_ U \to \mathcal{L}|_ U$. Then $s$ is a regular meromorphic section of $\mathcal{L}$ and we see that $\text{div}_\mathcal {L}(s) = nE$ for some $n \in \mathbf{Z}$ (Divisors, Definition <a href="/tag/0BE6" data-tag="0BE6">31.28.4</a>). By Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BE8" data-tag="0BE8">31.28.6</a> (and the fact that $X$ is normal by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AGR" data-tag="0AGR">54.3.2</a>) we conclude that $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{O}_ X(nE)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>